  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe approach focuses on minimizing the maximum cross-correlation using analytical gradients for the dominant term, avoiding finite differences. This method, combined with structured perturbations to escape local minima, aims to compute a better C\u2085 bound.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0, 1]^n_points, sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k (h \u22c6 (1 - h))[k] * dx\n- Gradient computation for dominant k: dC\u2085/dh[i] = (1 - h[i + k]) * dx if i +k < n_points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   i. Use initial_h_values if available; else, initialize with structured feasible h.\nb. Optimization loop:\n   while time budget not exhausted:\n      1. Compute C\u2085 for all lags.\n      2. Identify k_max where C\u2085 is maximum.\n      3. Compute gradient for k_max.\n      4. Update h using gradient descent.\n      5. Project h to [0,1] and scale to meet sum constraint.\n      6. Track improvements; apply perturbations on stagnation.\nc. Termination: Return best h.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Learning rate \u03b7: Start at 0.01, adjust based on improvement.\n- Perturbation strategy: Random Gaussian noise with adaptive scale.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Gradient-based updates ensure efficient convergence.\n- Periodic perturbations prevent stagnation.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h checked for constraints and evaluated with correlate().",
